# Agent behavior

## Working
- Never ask "are you ready to start?". Start.
- When a plan is confirmed, say it is done and wait for the next instruction.
- After a change, run the gates: `corepack pnpm check`, `corepack pnpm lint`, and the tests for the package you touched. Fix failures before reporting done.
- A claim of done needs native evidence: a screenshot of the app window and a clean console for the interaction. A unit test or a rendered component is not evidence.
- Never put the app in front of the user: launch it only in background mode, never `pnpm dev`, unless the user asks to watch (`AGENTS.md` Rule 5).

## Git
- Never run `git commit`, `git push`, or open a pull request unless the user asks in that conversation.
- Implementing a plan or working on a branch is not authorization to commit.
- Do not amend, rebase, reset, or force-push unless asked.

## Scratch files
- Transient files (PR body drafts, dumps, notes) live under `.context/`.
- Do not use `/tmp`, the repo root, or the home directory.

## Comments (non-negotiable, every language)
- Do not write comments in code. No narration, no rationale, no "why", no TODOs, no section headers.
- Applies to `//`, `/* */`, `#`, and every other comment syntax.
- The only exceptions:
  - JSDoc on an exported public API symbol where a consumer genuinely needs it.
  - A compiler or linter pragma the build requires (`eslint-disable-next-line`, `svelte-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`).
  - Svelte and HTML comments (`<!-- -->`) in `.svelte` markup.
  - A single-line `ponytail:` comment on a deliberate simplification, naming its ceiling and upgrade path (see `AGENTS.md`).
- When editing a file, remove any non-exception comment you meet. Do not keep it "for style".
- If a comment feels needed, the code is wrong. Rename, extract, or restructure until the code explains itself.
- If the user points out a comment you added, do not argue. Remove it.

## Severity
- Every lint rule is `error`. A warning is a rule that does not run.
